Cloudberry's gpcloud regression tests on the port, against the suite's S3,
on a cluster and on one node: the differences from Cloudberry's expected
output that have been reviewed, and are the port's for a reason of its own,
or the S3's.

Each file is one test's difference in the form ../../singlenode/canon.pl
gives it, named for the expected output it differs from; a file named
<test>.<target>.diff is the difference of one target alone, cluster or
one_node.  run.sh counts a test as passing when gpdiff.pl, under Cloudberry's
init file and the port's, finds no difference or exactly the file's.

  1_10_all_regions, 1_18_all_regions_version2
      psql 19's \d of a foreign table names its server, gp_exttable_server,
      where Cloudberry's gives its FDW options alone.  On one node the
      table's options say execute_on 'COORDINATOR_ONLY': the port reads
      every external table of one node there, as Cloudberry's single-node
      mode does (exttable_ddl.c).
  3_01_create_wet
      The test drops a table it never made, s3regress_create_wet_on_master,
      and Cloudberry's expected output says nothing of it; Cloudberry's
      server refuses it as the port does, a DROP of a foreign table that is
      not there.  Cloudberry's CI does not run gpcloud's regression tests
      (it needs Amazon's bucket and credentials), and the file is older than
      external tables as foreign tables.
  3_11_write_with_encryption
      The bucket s3test.encrypt.pivotal.io has a policy of Amazon's that
      refuses an upload without server-side encryption, which the test's
      third table makes; moto keeps the encryption's header of an upload but
      has no such policy, so the upload is taken and read back.  The rest --
      a table with sse_s3 written and read -- is Cloudberry's.
